Title: Keep hardware acceleration on Linux GPUs that only reach OpenGL ES 2.0
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

A GPU whose driver stops at OpenGL 2.1 -- Intel Ironlake and GM45, older
Radeons and GeForces, a good many laptops from before 2011 -- gives ANGLE an
ES 2.0 context and nothing higher. Chromium still ran on that in M144. It
now asks for ES 3.0 when it collects GPU info at startup and, on Linux, takes
a refusal as final: gpu::CollectGraphicsInfo() fails, GpuInit fails with it,
and the GPU process is replaced by software rendering. --ignore-gpu-blocklist
does not help, because this is not the blocklist.

The decision is features::ShouldFallbackToSWIfGLES3NotSupported(). On Windows
it is a feature that is off by default, with a comment that Direct3D 9 users
are still on ES 2 -- which is why the D3D9 backend that patch 11 restores
works at all. On ChromeOS it is a feature that is on. Everywhere else it is a
hardcoded true.

This gives Linux the Windows answer: false, so GLContextEGL is allowed to
fall back from ES 3 to ES 2, as it still knows how to. Everything past that
point already copes, since it is the path D3D9 takes: the compositor's and
raster's contexts ask for ES 3 with fallback allowed, WebGL 1 asks for ES 2,
and a WebGL 2 context fails to be created by itself ("ES3 is
blocklisted/disabled/unsupported by driver") without taking the process down.

Nothing changes on a GPU that has ES 3: the ES 3 context is created at the
first attempt and the fallback is never consulted.

diff --git a/ui/gl/gl_features.cc b/ui/gl/gl_features.cc
--- a/ui/gl/gl_features.cc
+++ b/ui/gl/gl_features.cc
@@ -283,6 +283,11 @@
   return is_enabled;
 #elif BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)
   return base::FeatureList::IsEnabled(kFallbackToSWIfGLES3NotSupported);
+#elif BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
+  // electron-legacy: a GPU that stops at OpenGL 2.1 gives ANGLE an ES 2.0
+  // context and no more. Take it, as Windows does for Direct3D 9 above,
+  // and do not send the whole GPU process to software for want of ES 3.
+  return false;
 #else   // BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
   return true;
 #endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
